{"name":"Colonnade Stone","hex":"#c6c0b6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"colonnade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A series of columns at regular intervals.","A peristyle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/colonnade"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Colonnade","blocks":[{"text":"In classical architecture, a colonnade is a long sequence of columns joined by their entablature, often free-standing, or part of a building. Paired or multiple pairs of columns are normally employed in a colonnade which can be straight or curved. The space enclosed may be covered or open. In St. Peter's Square in Rome, Bernini's great colonnade encloses a vast open elliptical space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg/330px-Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colonnade"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg/330px-Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg/960px-Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg/330px-Luxor_Temple_R07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALuxor_Temple_R07.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg/330px-Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg/960px-Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg/330px-Reconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_%283357410911%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReconstruction_of_Stoa_of_Attalos_(3357410911).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Palmyra%2C_Syria_-_2.jpg/330px-Palmyra%2C_Syria_-_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa